2026-07-29
----------
Phase 1 (Foundation) complete. Implemented per
outputs/2026-07-29-smart-console-plan.md, Tasks 1.1-1.9, in the order
1.1 -> 1.2 -> 1.3 -> 1.5 -> 1.6 -> 1.4 -> 1.7 -> 1.8 -> 1.9 (recorder/event
bus built before the connection manager that feeds them, and before the
Cisco plugin, per architect review).

Delivered:
- 3-crate Cargo workspace (smart-console-core / smart-console-ui /
  smart-console-cli), Rust edition 2024, stable toolchain 1.97.1.
- Config loader (toml + directories), device scanner (/dev/cu.* + USB
  VID/PID -> vendor name), connection manager (dedicated-thread <->
  tokio bridge, reconnect with backoff), event bus + line assembler,
  session recorder with redaction (passwords/secrets/SNMP community
  strings never hit disk unredacted; 0600/0700 permissions set at
  creation), VendorPlugin trait + PluginRegistry + Cisco IOS/IOS XE/NX-OS
  plugin, 4-pane ratatui TUI shell, CLI (`list-devices`, `connect`).
- 74 automated tests (58 core + 16 ui), all passing. `cargo fmt --check`,
  `cargo clippy --workspace --all-targets -- -D warnings`, and
  `cargo test --workspace` are all clean; the unwrap/expect/panic deny
  gate was verified to actually fire (not just declared) before Task 1.1
  was considered done.
- Real-machine verification: `list-devices` and `connect --help` run for
  real on this development machine; `connect` against a nonexistent port
  produces a clean error instead of a panic; the connection manager was
  exercised over a real Unix PTY pair (not just mocks).

Explicitly NOT verified (flagged rather than silently claimed complete):
- No physical USB-serial adapter or real network device (Cisco/Dell/
  Aruba/Comware/JunOS) was available in this environment. Device scanner,
  connection manager, and the Cisco plugin's banner/prompt parsing are
  unit-tested and PTY-tested but not confirmed against real hardware.
- No real attached terminal (TTY) was available to visually run the
  interactive `connect` + TUI flow; rendering is verified via
  `ratatui::TestBackend` snapshots instead of a manual on-screen run.
- Both gaps are called out in README.md's "Known limitations" section;
  please test against real hardware/a real terminal before production use.

Deviations from the original spec, documented and justified in
outputs/2026-07-29-smart-console-design.md:
- Log timestamps use UTC, not local time -- no date/time crate is in the
  mandated dependency list, and local-timezone conversion needs one (or
  unsafe FFI). Implemented a small dependency-free UTC calendar
  conversion instead (session/time_util.rs).
- `tokio-serial` was deliberately NOT added; the serial<->tokio bridge
  uses a dedicated OS thread + channel instead, staying within the
  mandated crate list.

Post-implementation review (advisor pass before declaring Phase 1 done)
found one real defect and two decisions worth recording explicitly:
- Ctrl+C while connected set a UI-internal flag that nothing downstream
  ever read, so it did nothing observable and the "second Ctrl+C quits
  once disconnected" fallback was unreachable. Fixed: the reader thread
  now publishes Disconnected on every exit path (not just the
  too-many-errors path), and a new disconnect_tx channel forwards the
  request from the UI to the CLI's ConnectionHandle. A regression test
  (`ctrl_c_while_connected_sends_on_disconnect_tx_not_just_the_app_flag`)
  now exercises the actual wiring, not just the App struct in isolation.
- Redaction patterns didn't cover key-based credential syntax
  (key-string, authentication key, radius-server/tacacs-server key).
  Added a deliberately broad `\bkey\b` pattern -- over-redaction is the
  stated safe default for this control.
- `create_secure_dir_all` originally trusted a pre-existing log_dir's
  permissions. First fix attempt over-corrected: it tightened every
  pre-existing ancestor directory, which broke in the test environment
  (can't chmod the system temp dir) -- caught by running the suite 3x,
  not a single green run. Corrected to tighten only the leaf log_dir,
  never its ancestors.
Full suite re-verified 3x clean after all three fixes: 58 core + 16 ui
tests, fmt/clippy clean.

Next: Phase 2 (Dell OS10/Aruba CX/Comware/JunOS plugins, real-time event
classification wired into the UI, persistent history + Ctrl-R search).

2026-07-29 (continued)
----------------------
Phase 2 (Multi-Vendor + Real-Time Parsing) complete. Implemented per
outputs/2026-07-29-smart-console-plan.md, Tasks 2.1-2.7, in the order
2.3 -> 2.4 -> 2.1 -> 2.2 -> (cross-vendor matrix) -> 2.5/2.6 -> 2.7
(Comware/JunOS first since their prompt grammars are the most different
from Cisco's, surfacing shared-helper design issues earlier; the matrix
test written immediately once all 5 plugins existed, before the detector
that depends on all of them).

Delivered:
- 4 new VendorPlugin implementations (HPE Comware, Juniper JunOS, Dell
  OS10, HPE Aruba CX), alongside Phase 1's Cisco plugin -- 5 total,
  registered in PluginRegistry::with_default_plugins().
- Cross-vendor detection matrix test: one banner fixture per vendor,
  asserts every plugin detects its own banner and no plugin detects any
  other vendor's banner.
- Detector module (core/src/detector.rs): consumes RawLine events,
  scans up to a 40-line banner window per connection before giving up
  (VendorDetectionStatus::Unknown), then dispatches every subsequent
  line to the detected plugin's parse_prompt/parse_output and publishes
  PromptChanged/Parsed/VendorDetection events. HostnameChanged is
  computed in the detector (by diffing consecutive PromptInfo.hostname
  values across parse_prompt calls), not inside any VendorPlugin -- no
  single plugin call has both the old and new hostname in scope at once,
  so this is the detector's job, not the plugin's.
- Live UI wiring: header now shows vendor/platform/version and current
  prompt mode from real SessionEvents (not placeholders); bottom-left
  Events pane renders real ParsedEvents with [ERROR]/[WARN]/[HOST]/[LINK]
  prefixes and theme colors.
- Persistent, redacted command history: smart_console_core::CommandHistory
  (0600 file, same redaction as session logs -- entries are redacted
  before being kept in memory or written to disk, so a past sensitive
  command can only ever show as [REDACTED], never recovered). Config
  gained history_path/history_max_entries fields with serde defaults, so
  a Phase-1-shaped config.toml missing these keys still loads (regression
  test: phase_1_shaped_toml_missing_history_fields_still_loads).
- Ctrl-R reverse-history-search in the UI (bash reverse-i-search style):
  first press enters search, repeated presses cycle to older matches,
  typing filters, Enter accepts the match into the input line without
  auto-submitting it, Esc cancels leaving input untouched.
- 147 automated tests (118 core + 29 ui at initial completion; 33 ui
  after the post-review fix pass below), all passing. fmt/clippy/test
  all clean, test suite run 3x to catch flakiness, release build
  verified.

Scope decision, stated explicitly (plan 2.6 asked for a shared
Error/Warning/LinkStatus/HostnameChange/Info classification across
vendors -- worth calling out plainly since 4 of 5 vendors classify
nothing beyond what's listed here):
- parse_output severity classification (Error/Warning) is implemented
  for Cisco (`%FACILITY-SEVERITY-...`) and Comware (`%%NN.../SEVERITY/...`)
  only, because both vendors' console message formats were confirmed
  against real documentation. LinkStatus is Cisco-only, for the same
  reason. JunOS, Dell OS10, and Aruba CX plugins return an empty Vec from
  parse_output: their console/syslog message formats were not confirmed
  precisely enough to classify without a real risk of misclassifying an
  unrelated line as an error or a link event. Guessing a format for a
  security-facing console tool was judged riskier than shipping "no
  classification for this vendor yet" -- this is a scope decision, not
  an oversight, and should be revisited once Phase 3 (or a user with
  real hardware) can confirm the actual formats.
- HostnameChanged is emitted only from the detector, never from a
  plugin's parse_output -- see the detector note above.
- There is no `Session` struct; connection/detector/recorder/history all
  key off the same EventBus + Config passed at connect() call sites
  (single-session CLI, per plan's Phase 1-2 scope).

Explicitly NOT verified against real hardware (same caveat as Phase 1's
Cisco plugin, now extended to all 4 new vendors):
- Comware, JunOS, Dell OS10, and Aruba CX banner/prompt/version fixtures
  are reconstructed from vendor documentation, not captured from or
  confirmed against real devices. The cross-vendor matrix test proves the
  5 fixtures don't collide with each other -- it does NOT prove any of
  them matches what a real device actually sends. Treat all green tests
  here as "internally consistent," not as "multi-vendor support
  confirmed." Please verify against real hardware before production use.

Post-implementation review (advisor pass before declaring Phase 2 done)
found two real defects in the Ctrl-R history-search UI, fixed in a
follow-up commit:
- Ctrl+C -- the app's only disconnect/quit path -- was unreachable while
  history search was active: on_key routed every key except Ctrl+R into
  the search-mode handler, which had no Ctrl+C case, so it fell into the
  generic Char arm and was typed into the query as a literal 'c'. Fixed
  by checking Ctrl+C before the search-mode dispatch (same as Ctrl+R),
  and having it cancel an active search on the same "get me out"
  principle.
- Ctrl+R with an empty history entered search mode anyway, producing a
  `(reverse-i-search)` line that could never show a match -- typing and
  Enter were both inert, only Esc/Ctrl+C escaped it. Fixed by showing a
  hint instead of entering search mode when history is empty.
- Also bounded app.history (previously unbounded, unlike scrollback/
  events) to MAX_HISTORY_ENTRIES via a new push_history() method, for
  consistency rather than a reported defect.
Full suite re-verified clean after the fixes: 118 core + 33 ui tests
(4 new regression tests), fmt/clippy clean.

Next: awaiting direction on Phase 3 (Advanced Features & Polish) --
tabs/split-view, command palette/search, config summary view, plugin
