2026-07-30
-----------
Phase 3 (Workflow & polish) in progress. Each task below landed as its own
commit with the full fmt/clippy -D warnings/test(x3) loop re-run clean
before moving to the next; test totals are cumulative core+ui+cli counts
at that point.

- **Task 3.1 -- multi-session tabs.** `App` now holds `sessions:
  Vec<Session>` + `active: usize` instead of a single session's fields
  directly; every field that used to live on `App` moved onto `Session`.
  `ttyt-core::events::SessionId` is a new newtype keying each session
  (assigned once from its `--port` position, never reused -- sessions are
  never removed at runtime this phase). `Ctrl+N` cycles the active tab; the
  left panel renders one line per session with the active one marked. The
  CLI's `connect()` now takes `Vec<String>` ports (repeatable `--port`),
  opens one `ConnectionHandle`/`CommandHistory`/recorder/detector per port,
  and tags each session's events onto one shared channel via a small
  per-session forwarder task (`tokio::select!` can't fan over a `Vec` of
  receivers, and stream-multiplexing crates are outside the mandated
  dependency list).
- **Task 3.4 -- confirm-before-send guard.** New `DangerousCommandGuard`
  (`ttyt-core::session::guard`) compiles `Config::dangerous_command_patterns`
  into regexes; Enter checks it before submitting and, on a match, enters a
  new `Mode::ConfirmSend(String)` instead. Only a plain (no Ctrl/Alt
  modifier) `y`/`Y` confirms; every other key declines and drops the
  command. Found and fixed a real bypass during self-review after this
  landed: the confirm handler originally matched on `KeyCode::Char('y')`
  alone, so Ctrl+Y (readline "yank") matched identically to a plain 'y' and
  silently confirmed a dangerous command -- fixed by also requiring no
  Ctrl/Alt modifier, with a regression test.
- **Decided ESC's Normal-mode behavior** before Task 3.2 could add a fourth
  `Mode` variant that would need to interact with it: clears `input` if
  non-empty, else shows a hint. (No Phase 3 task actually builds the "menu"
  ESC was originally reserved for in Phase 1's comments.)
- **Task 3.3 -- TAB autocomplete.** Every `VendorPlugin` gained a real
  `suggestions(&PromptInfo) -> Vec<String>` (previously an empty-default
  stub): Cisco/Dell OS10/Aruba CX share `plugin::common::cisco_style_
  suggestions` (same `>`/`#`/`(config)#` grammar); Comware and JunOS have
  their own vendor-specific verb tables. The detector computes suggestions
  from the same `parse_prompt` call that produces `PromptChanged` and
  publishes a new `SessionEvent::Suggestions(Vec<String>)` alongside it --
  kept in the detector (not given to `ttyt-ui` via its own `PluginRegistry`)
  so the UI crate never needs vendor logic. TAB inserts the next candidate
  into the input line and cycles on repeated presses; never auto-submits.
- **Task 3.2 -- command palette (`Ctrl+P`).** New `Mode::Palette
  (PaletteState)`; candidates are `Session::suggestions` (mode-dependent)
  + `Session::history` (most-recent-first), deduplicated. `fuzzy_matches`
  is a hand-rolled case-insensitive ordered-subsequence matcher (no fuzzy
  crate in the mandated dependency list). Ctrl+P is checked at the same
  priority as Ctrl+R/Ctrl+C, before mode dispatch. With this, the full
  keyboard map from the spec (Ctrl+C/N/P/L/R, TAB, ESC) is functional.
- **Fixed a real write-latency bug** found while chasing wildly variable
  (3.76s-78s) wall-clock time in a PTY-based integration test:
  `ConnectionHandle::write_line` and the reader thread's read loop contend
  for the same `Mutex<Box<dyn SerialTransport>>`; on an idle device (the
  common case) the reader holds the lock for the full ~200ms read-timeout
  cycle every iteration, releases it for nanoseconds, then immediately
  re-locks -- under macOS's unfair `os_unfair_lock` a competing
  `write_line` call could lose that race repeatedly and stall for seconds
  to tens of seconds before actually reaching the device. Fixed with an
  `Arc<AtomicUsize>` `write_pending` counter: `write_line` increments it
  before locking, and `reader_loop` sleeps 2ms after each read (outside the
  lock guard's scope) whenever a writer is waiting, guaranteeing it a
  window. Worst-case write latency is now bounded to roughly one read
  cycle. Regression test uses a 200ms-idle `MockTransport` cycle (matching
  the real device timeout) asserting `write_line` returns within 500ms
  under sustained reader contention.
- **Task 3.5 -- session replay.** New `ttyt replay <path> [--speed N]`
  subcommand (deliberately not a session type inside `connect()`: that
  command's select loop indexes `handles[id.index()]` per `--port`, so a
  replay tab with no matching `ConnectionHandle`/`CommandHistory` entry
  would panic on the first Enter press). `ttyt_core::session::replay::run`
  reads a saved log line by line and republishes each as
  `SessionEvent::RawLine` at a fixed interval, feeding the same
  detector/UI pipeline a live connection uses. Two format-driven decisions:
  `record_line` writes no per-line timestamp, so "adjustable speed" means a
  fixed lines-per-second rate, not fidelity to the original session's
  timing; and the recorder's `--- connection state: ... ---` marker lines
  replay as plain scrollback text rather than being re-parsed back into
  `ConnectionStateChanged` (no structured round-trip format exists for
  them) -- one side effect is a replay session's `connection_state` never
  leaves `Disconnected`, which conveniently makes a single Ctrl+C quit
  replay immediately via the existing `App::handle_ctrl_c` path, with no
  replay-specific handling needed. Required adding tokio's `fs` feature
  (only `io-util` was enabled before).
- **Task 3.10 -- Fortinet plugin stub.** `FortinetPlugin::detect` matches a
  loose banner token (`"FortiGate"`/`"FortiOS"`/`"Fortinet"`) and reports
  vendor "Fortinet", platform "not yet supported"; `parse_prompt`/
  `parse_output` are intentionally unimplemented. Registered **last** in
  `PluginRegistry::with_default_plugins` -- first-match-wins order means a
  loosely-token-matched stub must never get a chance to shadow a real
  vendor. The cross-vendor "no plugin detects another's banner" matrix test
  was extended from 5 to 6 fixtures to cover it.
- **Task 3.6 -- config summary skeleton.** Per the plan doc's own framing
  ("explicitly scoped as a future/experimental parser, not a completeness
  requirement... implement a minimal version and flag remaining work
  rather than stub it out silently"): `ttyt_core::config_summary::summarize`
  extracts hostname/interfaces/vlans from a generic Cisco-shaped config
  grammar. Deliberately **not** wired into the live pipeline or any UI
  widget -- the 4-pane layout has no free pane for it and inventing a
  fifth pane wasn't part of this task. Comware/JunOS's structurally
  different config syntax is known-unsummarized (empty-ish result, not an
  error).
- **Task 3.7 -- theme finalization.** `Config.theme` (already existed,
  defaulted to `"dark"`) was never actually read -- `ttyt_ui::run` hardcoded
  `Theme::dark()`. Added `Theme::from_name(&str)` (unrecognized names fall
  back to dark rather than refusing to start) and threaded it through as a
  real parameter to `ttyt_ui::run`, called from both `connect()` and
  `replay()`. Added a direct test for the design doc's "no rainbow colors"
  constraint (every palette field is grayscale or one of three meaningful
  accents) rather than relying on inspection alone. Only one theme exists;
  finalization here means "properly wired and verified," not "a second
  theme added."
- **Task 3.8 -- Linux groundwork (scanner).** The scanner's platform check
  used `cfg!(target_os = "macos")` directly, which meant a
  `#[cfg(target_os = "linux")]`-gated test would never run on this (or most
  contributors') macOS dev machine. Extracted `is_callout_device_for(name,
  target_os: &str)` as a pure predicate parameterized on the platform
  string, so both platforms' naming rules (`/dev/cu.*` vs
  `/dev/ttyUSB*`/`/dev/ttyACM*`, excluding bare `/dev/ttyS*`) are unit
  tested directly regardless of build platform. Untested against real
  Linux hardware/VM -- groundwork, not a completed port. The connection
  layer already had no macOS-specific path assumptions.
- **Task 3.9 -- Windows groundwork (design note only, no implementation).**
  Documented in README's "Known limitations": serial enumeration is nearly
  free (the `serialport` crate already returns COM ports; the scanner
  would just need a `"windows"` arm, likely permissive since Windows has no
  `cu.*`/`tty.*` duplicate-listing problem), and config/log/history paths
  are already Windows-safe via the `directories` crate. **The real gap is
  file/directory permissions**: `session::secure_fs`'s `0600`/`0700`
  guarantee is POSIX mode bits, `#[cfg(unix)]`-gated; the existing
  `#[cfg(not(unix))]` fallback compiles on Windows but creates
  files/directories with default, unrestricted permissions -- a real
  confidentiality gap on a shared machine, not cosmetic. A real Windows
  port needs a Windows ACL equivalent before it can honestly claim the same
  guarantee this project makes on macOS/Linux.

