2026-07-30 (continued) -- first public release
------------------------------------------------
Pushed to GitHub for the first time (public: github.com/zflow-byte/ttyt).
Before pushing: independent security review (security subagent), full
`git log -p --all` sweep for secrets across every commit (clean), added a
missing `LICENSE` (MIT, matching Cargo.toml's declared license), and
expanded `.gitignore` (editor dirs, swap files, defensive config/history
entries). Review found and fixed two real gaps: default redaction
patterns missed SNMPv3 auth/priv passphrases and VRRP/HSRP plaintext
auth (added `\bauth\b`/`\bpriv\b`/`\bauthentication\b`/`\bcleartext\b`),
and default dangerous-command patterns were Cisco-IOS-shaped only --
Comware's `reboot` and JunOS's `request system reboot`/`zeroize` sailed
past the confirm-before-send guard unconfirmed (added all three).
Regression tests added for both directly against `Redactor`/
`DangerousCommandGuard`. README's status line lost its reference to
`outputs/2026-07-29-smart-console-plan.md` "in the parent workspace" --
that file lives in a private multi-project workspace this repo was
extracted from, not something that ships with the public repo.

Tagged and pushed `v0.1.0`. Filled in `Formula/ttyt.rb`'s real GitHub
path and, once the tag existed, its real `sha256` (was a placeholder) --
verified by actually building and testing from the real release tarball,
not just hashing it. Published a proper tap
(github.com/zflow-byte/homebrew-ttyt) so `brew tap zflow-byte/ttyt &&
brew install ttyt` works as a real single-command install; along the way
discovered current Homebrew (6.x) rejects installing a bare formula file
outside a tap, and requires one-time `brew trust` for a third-party tap
before its formulae will load -- both documented in README. Also found
and fixed a PATH-shadow conflict on the test machine: `~/.cargo/bin`
(from earlier `cargo install --path crates/ttyt-cli` testing this
session) sat ahead of Homebrew's bin directory, silently shadowing the
Homebrew-installed binary -- removed the cargo-installed copy so `ttyt`
resolves to the Homebrew one, and documented the gotcha in README for
anyone in the same situation.

**Real bug found via actual hardware testing (v0.1.0), fixed in v0.1.1:**
`widgets/console.rs` built its scrollback+input-line content as a
`Paragraph`, which top-aligns by default. With little/no scrollback (a
freshly connected or quiet device -- exactly the common case right after
`connect`), the rendered lines were far shorter than the pane's height,
so the input line rendered a few rows below the pane's top border while
the reported terminal cursor position stayed calculated as the pane's
actual last row regardless -- a visible gap between the "> " prompt text
and the blinking cursor, with no way to tell what mode the session was in
or how much had been typed before Enter would submit it. Existing test
coverage never caught this because `widgets::mod::tests::render_to_string`
flattens the entire screen buffer into one string before asserting,
checking only that content appears *somewhere*, never *which row*.

Fixed by padding `lines` with leading blank lines up to the pane's full
height before building the `Paragraph`, so the input line always lands
on the actual last row, matching how a real terminal fills upward from
the bottom rather than top-aligning. Added a real regression test
(`input_line_renders_on_the_pane_last_row_even_with_no_scrollback`) that
checks row *position* directly via `TestBackend`'s buffer content at a
specific row -- confirmed it actually catches the bug by reverting the
fix locally and re-running (test failed as expected: cursor row was
blank), then restoring it (test passed).

Bumped workspace version 0.1.0 -> 0.1.1 (Cargo.toml + Cargo.lock), tagged
and pushed `v0.1.1`, re-verified full fmt/clippy -D warnings/test (3x)
clean: 222 tests (152 core + 67 ui + 3 cli). Formula/ttyt.rb's `url`/
`sha256` updated to point at the real v0.1.1 tarball -- v0.1.0 remains
tagged in git history as the record of what was actually reviewed and
published first, not deleted or force-moved.
